You have been making API calls from Python. Today you make them from the browser using JavaScript. This matters because most of the world interacts with data through web pages, not terminal windows. When you can fetch data and display it on a page, you can build things people will actually use.

By the end of today you will have
1. Made an API call from the browser using fetch()
2. Handled the response using async and await
3. Displayed live API data on a web page using DOM manipulation
4. Added a loading state and an error state so the page never breaks

Python vs JavaScript for API Calls
You already know how to call APIs in Python. JavaScript does the same thing, with slightly different syntax and one important rule: browser JavaScript is asynchronous by default.

Python (requests)
Runs in the terminal. Waits for the response before moving on. Simpler to read for beginners.

JavaScript (fetch)
Runs in the browser. Does not wait for responses by default. Requires async/await to write it clearly.

The concepts are the same. A URL, a request, a response, JSON data, error handling. The syntax is different. The environment is different. That is all.

What is async/await?
The browser does not freeze while waiting for an API response. It keeps the page responsive and handles the response when it arrives. This is called asynchronous behaviour.

async/await is JavaScript syntax that lets you write asynchronous code in a way that reads like normal step-by-step code. Without it, you would need to chain .then() callbacks. With it, you write await before the request and the code reads in order, even though it is non-blocking.

You will write functions with the async keyword. Inside them, you put await before any call that takes time to complete, like a fetch request. This tells JavaScript to wait for that step before moving to the next line, but without freezing the browser.

Your First Browser API Call
Create a new file called day4_fetch.html. This is a complete web page with HTML and JavaScript in one file:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Country Info</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 600px; margin: 40px auto; padding: 0 20px; }
    h1 { color: #1F4E79; }
    #result { background: #f7f9fc; border: 1px solid #d0dff0; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px; margin-top: 20px; }
    button { background: #2E75B6; color: white; border: none; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 5px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 16px; }
    button:hover { background: #1F4E79; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Country Info</h1>
<input type="text" id="countryInput" placeholder="Enter a country name" style="padding:8px; font-size:16px; width:250px;">
<button onclick="loadCountry()">Search</button>
<div id="result">Enter a country name above and click Search.</div>

<script>
async function loadCountry() {
  const country = document.getElementById("countryInput").value.trim();

  if (!country) {
    document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = "Please enter a country name.";
    return;
  }

  document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = "Loading...";

  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://restcountries.com/v3.1/name/${country}`);

    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Country not found (${response.status})`);
    }

    const data = await response.json();
    const info = data[0];

    const name = info.name.common;
    const capital = info.capital ? info.capital[0] : "N/A";
    const population = info.population.toLocaleString();
    const region = info.region;
    const flag = info.flags.emoji;

    document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = `
      <h2>${flag} ${name}</h2>
      <p><strong>Capital:</strong> ${capital}</p>
      <p><strong>Region:</strong> ${region}</p>
      <p><strong>Population:</strong> ${population}</p>
    `;
  } catch (error) {
    document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = `Error: ${error.message}`;
  }
}
</script>

</body>
</html>
Open this file in your browser (double-click it or drag it into Chrome). Search for Kenya, then Nigeria, then try a name that does not exist. Notice how the loading state and error state both work cleanly.

Understanding What Happened
Walk through the key parts:

async function loadCountry(): the async keyword tells JavaScript this function will do something that takes time. Inside it, you can use await.

await fetch(...): sends the request. The function pauses here until the response arrives, but the browser stays responsive. fetch returns a Response object, not the data itself yet.

if (!response.ok): response.ok is true for status codes 200-299. If the request failed (404, 500, etc.), you throw an error which the catch block handles.

await response.json(): reading the JSON body is also asynchronous, so it needs its own await.

try { ... } catch (error) { ... }: this is JavaScript's error handling. If anything inside try throws an error, it jumps to catch instead of crashing the page.

The Loading State
Before the request starts, you set the result div to "Loading...". This matters because API calls can take a second or more. Without a loading indicator, the user has no feedback and may click the button again. Setting the text to "Loading..." before the await call gives immediate feedback.

Always show a loading state for any async operation. "Loading..." costs nothing to add. A page that appears frozen costs you users.

API Keys in the Browser
Notice that today's example uses a public API with no key. This is intentional. When you put an API key in browser JavaScript, it is visible to anyone who views your page source. Anyone can copy it.

Do not put API keys in browser JavaScript. If you need to call an authenticated API from a web page, you call your own backend (a Python Flask server, for example) and the backend holds the key. The browser calls your backend. Your backend calls the external API. The key never reaches the browser. You will build this pattern on Day 16.

Today's Exercise
Build a web page that fetches and displays live data with a loading indicator and an error state.

Requirements:

Uses fetch() with async/await
Shows "Loading..." before the request completes
Displays clean, formatted output when the request succeeds
Shows a clear error message when the request fails (test this by entering a bad value)
The page should look reasonable in a browser, not just raw text
Pick any public API that does not need a key. REST Countries, Open-Meteo, and CoinGecko all work from the browser. Push to GitHub.

Outcome: You can integrate live API data into a web page using JavaScript's Fetch API. You handle the loading state, the success state, and the error state correctly.
